#{[17592186326925 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}k & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =k\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =k(0\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 2)-2(3\\cdot 3-4\\cdot 2)+2(3\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =-2k-2+6 \\\\  & =-2k+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =0 \\\\ -2k+4 & =0 \\\\ 2k & =4 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=2.$"] [17592186326926 "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =((-4)\\cdot 5-2\\cdot 1)+2(2\\cdot 5-0\\cdot 1)+(2\\cdot 2-0\\cdot (-4)) \\\\  & =-22+20+4 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is\n\n$$\nV=|2|=2.\n$$"] [17592186326928 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/6d/6d4f13abad5cc193dccedee3114fbd9cbd9fb8e6b5b3ddb0d07f0ed2ed51f4ff.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∮}_{C}x\\operatorname{cos}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x+x\\operatorname{sin}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le y+1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 0\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{y+1}(1+x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\text{ }dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186329713 "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta (\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }) & =\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }+\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta {\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }+\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }}+\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }} \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we simplified the right-hand side using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })=1.\n$$"] [17592186329714 "We put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{1-\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }+\\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{(1-\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }+\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )(1-\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta (1-\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{(1-\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )}^{2}}{(1-\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{(1-\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )}^{2}}{(1-\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}\\theta } & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +1-2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }{(1-\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta )+1-2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{(1-\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1+1-2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{(1-\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{2-2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{(1-\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )}{(1-\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta } \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sec}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\n$$"] [17592186329716 "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }({\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\\cdot {\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta +\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\\cdot {\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }+\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\cdot \\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }}+\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }} \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we simplified the right-hand side using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }({\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta )=1.\n$$"] [17592186329718 "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}t(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}t}{\\operatorname{sin}t}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}t}{\\operatorname{cos}t}) & =\\operatorname{sin}t\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}t}{\\operatorname{sin}t}+\\operatorname{sin}t\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}t}{\\operatorname{cos}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}t\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}t}{\\operatorname{sin}t}+\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t}{\\operatorname{cos}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}t\\cdot \\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{sin}t}}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{sin}t}}+\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t}{\\operatorname{cos}t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}t+\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t}{\\operatorname{cos}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t}{\\operatorname{cos}t} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}t}{1}+\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t}{\\operatorname{cos}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}t}{1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}t}+\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t}{\\operatorname{cos}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t}{\\operatorname{cos}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify using the Pythagorean identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t}{\\operatorname{cos}t} &=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}t}=\\operatorname{sec}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$"] [17592186329720 "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{ cm}, \\\\ b & =AC=\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $m∠A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\cancel{\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}} & =\\cancel{\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ 1 & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡A & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ m∠A & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186329721 "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=5\\,\\text{ cm}, \\\\ b & =AC=12\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $m∠A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathcal{A} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}A \\\\ 15 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 12\\cdot 5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}A \\\\ 15 & =30\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}A \\\\ \\frac{15}{30} & =\\operatorname{sin}A \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}A & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ m\\mathrm{∠}A & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186332914 "The diagonal $\\overset{―}{AC}$ divides the parallelogram into two congruent triangles. Therefore, the area of the parallelogram is twice the area of $△ACD$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/a6/a60b049aef53aef28802b084b3e445fd6253978500ca981d3b799977523cdd71.png)\n\n Computing the area of $△ACD,$ we find\n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{ACD} & =\\frac{1}{2}(8)(5)\\operatorname{sin}{55}^{∘} \\\\  & =20\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{55}^{∘} \\\\  & \\approx 16.383,\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. Consequently,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{ABCD} & =2{\\mathcal{A}}_{ACD} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 16.383 \\\\  & =32.766.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, to the nearest integer, the area of the parallelogram is $33.$"] [17592186335769 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(1,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,1) & =2(1+1)-2=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,1) & ={f}_{x}(1,1)(x-1) \\\\ z-1 & =2(x-1) \\\\ z-1 & =2x-2 \\\\ z & =2x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,1,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=2x-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=2x-1$ (or $2x-z-1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{2}=⟨2,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨2,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-i-2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $-\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(-\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})=\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is also parallel to it."] [17592186335800 "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n∇[fg]=g\\,∇\\,f+f\\,∇\\,g\n$$\n\nSo, using this product rule we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}[f(x,y)g(x,y)] & =g(x,y)\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)+f(x,y)\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}(xy))(y\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+x\\text{ }\\mathbf{j})+xy(\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =y\\operatorname{ln}(xy)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+x\\operatorname{ln}(xy)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}+y\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+x\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =[y\\operatorname{ln}(xy)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+y\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}]+[x\\operatorname{ln}(xy)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}+x\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}] \\\\  & =y(\\operatorname{ln}(xy)+1)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+x(\\operatorname{ln}(xy)+1)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186335802 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y}=\\frac{2\\cdot 0+0}{3\\cdot 0+0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$” is false. Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=x}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x+x}{3x+x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3x}{4x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $-\\frac{1}{2}$ along the path $y=-x$” is true. Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=-x}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-2x+x}{3x-x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-x}{2x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit exists and is equal to $0$” is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"] [17592186335804 "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,1,-1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(-1,1,-1).$  \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$  and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(-1,1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}z)\\,k \\\\  & =({y}^{2}+2xz)\\text{ }i+(2xy+{z}^{2})\\text{ }j+(2yz+{x}^{2})\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(-1,1,-1) & =((1)^{2}+2(-1)(-1))\\,i+(2(-1)(1)+(-1)^{2}))\\,j+(2(1)(-1)+(-1)^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =3\\,i-1j-1\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,1,-1).$"] [17592186335809 "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}t(1-\\operatorname{cos}t)-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t & =\\operatorname{cos}t-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}t-({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}t-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we simplified the right-hand side using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t=1.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}t(1-\\operatorname{cos}t)-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t=\\operatorname{cos}t-1.\n$$"] [17592186335810 "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}t(\\operatorname{sin}t-1)+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t-\\operatorname{sin}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t \\\\  & =({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t)-\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we simplified the right-hand side using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t=1.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}t(\\operatorname{sin}t-1)+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t=1-\\operatorname{sin}t.\n$$"] [17592186335811 "First, we factor $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ from the expression:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}\\theta {\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\cdot ({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta )\n$$\n\nThen, we use ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\cdot ({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{cos}^{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}\\theta {\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta .\n$$"] [17592186335815 "First, we factor $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ from the expression:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}x{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}x\\operatorname{cos}x=\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot ({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x)\n$$\n\nThen, we use ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot ({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x) & =\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}x{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}x\\operatorname{cos}x=\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x.\n$$"] [17592186335816 "First, we factor ${\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}x$ from the expression:\n\n$$\n{\\operatorname{sin}}^{5}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}x={\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}x\\cdot ({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x)\n$$\n\nThen, we use ${\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}x\\cdot ({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x) & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\operatorname{sin}}^{5}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}x={\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}x.\n$$"] [17592186335817 "First, we factor ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x$  from the first two terms:\n\n$$\n{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{4}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x\\cdot ({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x)+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x\n$$\n\nThen, we use ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x=1$ to simplify the given expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x\\cdot ({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x)+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x\\cdot 1+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{4}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x=1.\n$$"] [17592186335818 "First, we factor the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}t{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}t}{\\operatorname{cos}t}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}t\\cdot ({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t)}{\\operatorname{cos}t}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}t\\cdot ({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t)}{\\operatorname{cos}t} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}t\\cdot 1}{\\operatorname{cos}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}t}{\\operatorname{cos}t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}t{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}t}{\\operatorname{cos}t}=\\operatorname{tan}t.\n$$"] [17592186335819 "First, we factor the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta {\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta }=\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot ({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot ({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )} & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta {\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta }=\\operatorname{cot}\\theta .\n$$"] [17592186335820 "First, we factor the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{4}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\cdot ({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\cdot ({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{4}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }=\\operatorname{tan}\\theta .\n$$"] [17592186335821 "We put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x}+\\operatorname{sin}x & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{1} \\\\  & \\phantom{=}\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x(\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x)}{1(\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+\\operatorname{sin}x(\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x)}{\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+\\operatorname{sin}x(\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x)}{\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x} & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x+\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x)+\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x}+\\operatorname{sin}x=\\frac{1+\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x+\\operatorname{cos}x}.\n$$"] [17592186335939 "The area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is the sum of the areas of the triangles $△ABD$ and $△BDC$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/68/68f5708d49577befe9bac6a7aaf6a54fcdb30dcd344367d2c69b8924093dbb61.png)\n\n \n\nFirst we find the area for $△ABD:$ \n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 12\\cdot 14\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{42.1}^{∘} \\\\  & =84\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{42.1}^{∘} \\\\  & \\approx 56.315\\hspace{0.167em}8,\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nNow we find the area for $△BCD:$ \n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{BDC} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 7\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{103.8}^{∘} \\\\  & =17.5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{103.8}^{∘} \\\\  & \\approx 16.994\\hspace{0.167em}8,\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nFinally, we add together the areas of our two triangles to determine the area of $ABCD:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{ABCD} & ={\\mathcal{A}}_{ABD}+{\\mathcal{A}}_{BDC} \\\\  & =56.315\\hspace{0.167em}8+16.994\\hspace{0.167em}8 \\\\  & =73.310\\hspace{0.167em}6\\end{aligned}$$\n\nConsequently, to the nearest tenth, the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $73.3.$"] [17592186335940 "The area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is the sum of the areas of the triangles $△ACD$ and $△ABC$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/7e/7e3232258a523e4dbfebb6c380f5b88c2a54036b4d719e70ebf9060054f825d7.png)\n\n \n\nFirst we find the area for $△ACD:$ \n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{ACD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 18\\cdot 21\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{60}^{∘} \\\\  & =189\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{60}^{∘} \\\\  & \\approx 163.679,\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nNow we find the area for $△ABC:$ \n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{ABC} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 36\\cdot 30\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{33}^{∘} \\\\  & =540\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{33}^{∘} \\\\  & \\approx 294.105,\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nFinally, we add together the areas of our two triangles to determine the area of $ABCD:$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{ABCD} & ={\\mathcal{A}}_{ACD}+{\\mathcal{A}}_{ABC} \\\\  & =163.679+294.105 \\\\  & =457.784\\end{aligned}$$\n\nConsequently, to the nearest integer, the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $458.$"] [17592186338805 "Using the constant multiple rule and the sum rule, we have\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{f}(t)+4\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+4{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}t),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{1}{t},\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\hspace{0.222em}4{t}^{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}(t)+4\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'} & ={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+4{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{1}{t},\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩+4⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\hspace{0.222em}4{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{1}{t},\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩+⟨4,\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{4}{{t}^{2}},\\hspace{0.222em}16{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}+4,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{1}{t}-\\frac{4}{{t}^{2}},\\hspace{0.222em}0+16{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1+8\\sqrt{t}}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{t-4}{{t}^{2}},\\hspace{0.222em}16{t}^{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338806 "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(u(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{'}={u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t)+u(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's find ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2t \\\\ 3{t}^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}t)=\\frac{1}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{'} & ={u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t)+u(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ {t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}+\\operatorname{ln}t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2t \\\\ 3{t}^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ t \\\\ {t}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}t \\\\ 2t\\operatorname{ln}t \\\\ 3{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1+\\operatorname{ln}t \\\\ t+2t\\operatorname{ln}t \\\\ {t}^{2}+3{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338894 "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{WX}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{WY}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{WX} & =\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{w} \\\\  & =(-2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(2\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-4\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-4,4,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{WY} & =\\mathbf{y}-\\mathbf{w} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(2\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨1,2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{WX}\\times \\overset{\\to }{WY} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -4 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(4\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{i}-((-4)\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{j}+((-4)\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 4)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =6\\mathbf{i}+9\\mathbf{j}-12\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨6,9,-12⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{WX}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{WY}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{WX}\\times \\overset{\\to }{WY}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{9}^{2}+{12}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{261}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,WXY$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{261}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186338895 "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{NP}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{NQ}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{NP} & =\\mathbf{p}-\\mathbf{n} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨1,2,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{NQ} & =\\mathbf{q}-\\mathbf{n} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨1,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{NP}\\times \\overset{\\to }{NQ} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{i}-(1\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{j}+(1\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,2,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{NP}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{NQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{NP}\\times \\overset{\\to }{NQ}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,NPQ$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186338896 "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{FG}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{FH}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{FG} & =\\mathbf{g}-\\mathbf{f} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{FH} & =\\mathbf{h}-\\mathbf{f} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{FG}\\times \\overset{\\to }{FH} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(1\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{i}-(1\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{j}+(1\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,1,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{FG}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{FH}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{FG}\\times \\overset{\\to }{FH}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,FGH$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186338898 "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{CD}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{CE}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{CD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{c} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{CE} & =\\mathbf{e}-\\mathbf{c} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{j}-2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,3,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{CD}\\times \\overset{\\to }{CE} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{i}-(0\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{j}+(0\\cdot 3-0\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i} \\\\  & =⟨2,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{CD}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{CE}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{CD}\\times \\overset{\\to }{CE}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,CDE$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=1.\n$$"] [17592186338899 "Note that the length of the altitude $MH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,LMN$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN\n$$\n\nSo, to find $MH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM} & =\\mathbf{m}-\\mathbf{l} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =\\mathbf{n}-\\mathbf{l} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{i}-((-2)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{j}+((-2)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot (-2))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,LMN$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2}=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $LN$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}LN & =|\\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} & =MH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ MH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ MH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338900 "Note that the length of the altitude $VH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,TUV$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}VH\\cdot TU\n$$\n\nSo, to find $VH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{TU}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{TV}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{TU} & =\\mathbf{u}-\\mathbf{t} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{j}-(3\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-3,-1,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{TV} & =\\mathbf{v}-\\mathbf{t} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{TU}\\times \\overset{\\to }{TV} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -3 & -1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot (-3))\\mathbf{i}-((-3)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot (-3))\\mathbf{j}+((-3)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,0,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{TU}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{TV}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{TU}\\times \\overset{\\to }{TV}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,TUV$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2}=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $TU$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TU & =|\\overset{\\to }{TU}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}VH\\cdot TU,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}VH\\cdot TU \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}VH\\cdot \\sqrt{19} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} & =VH\\cdot \\sqrt{19} \\\\ VH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{19}} \\\\ VH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{38}}{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338901 "Note that the length of the altitude $ZH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,WYZ$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}ZH\\cdot WY\n$$\n\nSo, to find $ZH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{WY}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{WZ}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{WY} & =\\mathbf{y}-\\mathbf{w} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(2\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{WZ} & =\\mathbf{z}-\\mathbf{w} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(2\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}-4\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,-4,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{WY}\\times \\overset{\\to }{WZ} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -4 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot (-1)-(-4)\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{i}-((-1)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{j}+((-1)\\cdot (-4)-(-1)\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-3,0,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{WY}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{WZ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{WY}\\times \\overset{\\to }{WZ}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,WYZ$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $WY$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}WY & =|\\overset{\\to }{WY}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}ZH\\cdot WY,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}ZH\\cdot WY \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}ZH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2} & =ZH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ ZH & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ ZH & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338902 "Note that the length of the altitude $GH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,GSM$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}GH\\cdot SM\n$$\n\nSo, to find $GH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{SG}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{SM}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{SG} & =\\mathbf{g}-\\mathbf{s} \\\\  & =(-2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨-3,1,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{SM} & =\\mathbf{m}-\\mathbf{s} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})-(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{j}-2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,2,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{SG}\\times \\overset{\\to }{SM} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(1\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{i}-((-3)\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{j}+((-3)\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}-6\\mathbf{j}-6\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,-6,-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{SG}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{SM}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{SG}\\times \\overset{\\to }{SM}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-6)^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{76} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,GSM$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{19}}{2}=\\sqrt{19}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $SM$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}SM & =|\\overset{\\to }{SM}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}GH\\cdot SM,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}GH\\cdot SM \\\\ \\sqrt{19} & =\\frac{1}{2}GH\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{19} & =GH\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\ GH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ GH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{38}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338904 "Note that the length of the altitude $DH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,DMA$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}DH\\cdot MA\n$$\n\nSo, to find $DH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{MD}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{MA}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{MD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{m} \\\\  & =(-\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(-\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,2,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{MA} & =\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{m} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(-\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{MD}\\times \\overset{\\to }{MA} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 2-(-2)\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{i}-(0\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{j}+(0\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =8\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}-4\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨8,4,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{MD}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{MA}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{MD}\\times \\overset{\\to }{MA}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{8}^{2}+{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{96} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,DMA$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{4\\sqrt{6}}{2}=2\\sqrt{6}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $MA$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}MA & =|\\overset{\\to }{MA}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}DH\\cdot MA,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}DH\\cdot MA \\\\ 2\\sqrt{6} & =\\frac{1}{2}DH\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{6} & =DH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ DH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ DH & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338918 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-2,-6).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-2,-6):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-2,-6) & =\\frac{-6-(-2)}{\\sqrt{(-2)(-6+(-2))}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{4} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,-6) & ={f}_{y}(-2,-6)(y-(-6)) \\\\ z-1 & =-1(y+6) \\\\ z-1 & =-y-6 \\\\ z & =-y-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,-6,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2 \\\\ z &=-y-5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-2,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=-y-5$ (or $y+z+5=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{2}=⟨0,1,1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,-1,1⟩.$"] [17592186338919 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(-1,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-1,1) & =3{e}^{-1+1}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(-1)\\cdot 1| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =3-0 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-1,1) & ={f}_{x}(-1,1)(x-(-1)) \\\\ z-(-1) & =3(x+1) \\\\ z+1 & =3x+3 \\\\ z & =3x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-1,1,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=3x+2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=3x+2$ (or $3x-z+2=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{2}=⟨3,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨3,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-i-3\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-3⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-3⟩=⟨1,0,3⟩$ is also parallel to it."] [17592186338920 "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n{(u(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))}^{'}={u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t)+u(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's find ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5t+6)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-3)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))}^{'} & ={u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t)+u(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =2\\begin{bmatrix}4t-1 \\\\ 3t+2 \\\\ 5t+6\\end{bmatrix}+(2t-3)\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8t-2 \\\\ 6t+4 \\\\ 10t+12\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}8t-12 \\\\ 6t-9 \\\\ 10t-15\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}16t-14 \\\\ 12t-5 \\\\ 20t-3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338921 "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n{(u(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))}^{'}={u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t)+u(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's find ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})=2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))}^{'} & ={u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t)+u(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =2t\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 8 \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}+{t}^{2}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2t{e}^{t} \\\\ 16t \\\\ 2t{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -{t}^{2}{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(2t+{t}^{2}){e}^{t} \\\\ 16t \\\\ (2t-{t}^{2}){e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338922 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-1,0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-1,0) & =\\frac{(-1)(0-(-1))}{2(-1)(0+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-1,0) & ={f}_{y}(-1,0)(y-0) \\\\ z-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}y \\\\ z & =\\frac{1}{2}y+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-1,0,\\frac{1}{2})$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-1 \\\\ z &=\\frac{1}{2}y+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=\\frac{1}{2}y+\\frac{1}{2}$ (or $y-2z+1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${\\mathbf{n}}_{2}=⟨0,1,-2⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\,j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,2,1⟩.$"] [17592186338929 "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$, can be found by computing $∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}) & =2\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\text{ }i+2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{∇}f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})}{‖\\mathrm{∇}f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})‖} & =\\frac{-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j})\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338930 "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(\\frac{3}{2},1)$, can be found by computing $∇f(\\frac{3}{2},1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(\\frac{3}{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+3y-2xy)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+3y-2xy)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(1-2y)\\text{ }i+(3-2x)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{3}{2},1) & =(1-2(1))\\text{ }i+(3-2\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(\\frac{3}{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{∇}f(\\frac{3}{2},1)}{‖\\mathrm{∇}f(\\frac{3}{2},1)‖} & =\\frac{-\\mathbf{i}}{1}=-\\mathbf{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338931 "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(0,0)$, can be found by computing $∇f(0,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(0,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+y}+x+y)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+y}+x+y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =({e}^{x+y}+1)\\text{ }i+({e}^{x+y}+1)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(0,0) & =({e}^{0+0}+1)\\text{ }i+({e}^{0+0}+1)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =2i+2j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(0,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{∇}f(0,0)}{‖\\mathrm{∇}f(0,0)‖} & =\\frac{2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}}{\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338932 "If we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=4.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i+2y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,0) & =2(2)\\,i+2(0)\\,j \\\\  & =4\\,i \\\\  & =⟨4,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(2,0)=⟨4,0⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-{x}_{0}){v}_{y}-(y-{y}_{0}){v}_{x} & =0 \\\\ (x-2)0-(y-0)4 & =0 \\\\ -4y & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338933 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+y-1 & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\ -1 & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}-2x-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}-2x-y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-1.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}-2x-y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}-2x-y)\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-2)\\,i+(-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-1)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,0) & =(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}-{0}^{2}}}-2)\\,i+(-\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}-{0}^{2}}}-1)\\,j \\\\  & =-i-j \\\\  & =⟨-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(1,0)=⟨-1,-1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(1,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{-1} & =\\frac{y-0}{-1} \\\\ 1-x & =-y \\\\ x-y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338934 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2} & =4xy \\\\ {y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4xy & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,-3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-1,-3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-1,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-4xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(6x-4y)\\,i+(2y-4x)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-1,-3) & =(6(-1)-4(-3))\\,i+(2(-3)-4(-1))\\,j \\\\  & =6\\,i-2\\,j \\\\  & =⟨6,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(-1,-3)=⟨6,-2⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(-1,-3).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(-1)}{6} & =\\frac{y-(-3)}{-2} \\\\ (-2)(x+1) & =6(y+3) \\\\ -2x-2 & =6y+18 \\\\ 6y+2x+20 & =0 \\\\ 3y+x+10 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338936 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y) & =2xy \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2xy & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{x+y}-2y)\\,i+(\\frac{1}{x+y}-2x)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(0,1) & =(\\frac{1}{0+1}-2(1))\\,i+(\\frac{1}{0+1}-2(0))\\,j \\\\  & =-i+j \\\\  & =⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(0,1)=⟨-1,1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(0,1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-0}{-1} & =\\frac{y-1}{1} \\\\ -x & =y-1 \\\\ x+y-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338938 "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(3,4,0)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(3,4,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(3,4,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}})\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\text{ }i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\text{ }j+\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(3,4,0) & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(4)^{2}+(0)^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(4)^{2}+(0)^{2}}}\\,j+\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(4)^{2}+(0)^{2}}}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{4}{5}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{3}{5}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{4}{5}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(3,4,0).$"] [17592186338939 "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-2,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2}-xy)-yz)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2}-xy)-yz)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2}-xy)-yz)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{{z}^{2}-xy}\\text{ }i+(-\\frac{x}{{z}^{2}-xy}-z)\\text{ }j+(\\frac{2z}{{z}^{2}-xy}-y)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-2,0) & =-\\frac{(-2)}{(0)^{2}-(1)(-2)}\\,i+(-\\frac{1}{(0)^{2}-(1)(-2)}-0)\\,j+(\\frac{2(0)}{(0)^{2}-(1)(-2)}-(-2))\\,k \\\\  & =i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{1}{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0).$"] [17592186338940 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-xz & =6-3xyz \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-xz+3xyz & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=3{x}^{2}-xz+3xyz$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=6.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(1,0,-3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,0,-3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,0,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-xz+3xyz)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}-xz+3xyz)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3{x}^{2}-xz+3xyz)k \\\\  & =(6x-z+3yz)\\text{ }i+3xz\\text{ }j+(-x+3xy)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,0,-3) & =(6(1)-(-3)+3(0)(-3))\\text{ }i+3(1)(-3)\\text{ }j+(-1+3(1)(0))\\text{ }k \\\\  & =9\\,i-9\\,j-k \\\\  & =⟨9,-9,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y-0}{-9}=\\frac{z-(-3)}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y}{-9}=\\frac{z+3}{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338941 "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)) \\\\  & =y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z) \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z) \\\\ {h}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)) \\\\  & =xy\\cdot \\frac{1}{z} \\\\  & =\\frac{xy}{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}h(x,y,z) & ={h}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+{h}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}+{h}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =y\\operatorname{ln}(z)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+x\\operatorname{ln}(z)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{xy}{z}\\text{ }\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338942 "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+{e}^{xyz}) \\\\  & =2x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+yz{e}^{xyz} \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+{e}^{xyz}) \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}y{z}^{2}+xz{e}^{xyz} \\\\ {g}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+{e}^{xyz}) \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+xy{e}^{xyz}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,  the gradient is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}g(x,y,z) & ={g}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+{g}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}+{g}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+yz{e}^{xyz})\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+(2{x}^{2}y{z}^{2}+xz{e}^{xyz})\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}+(2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+xy{e}^{xyz})\\text{ }\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338952 "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $h(x,y)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n∇[fh]=h\\,∇\\,f+f\\,∇\\,h\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}[f(x,y)h(x,y)] & =h(x,y)\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)+f(x,y)\\mathrm{∇}h(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{xy}((2xy+{y}^{2})\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+({x}^{2}+2xy)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j})+({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{1}{x{y}^{2}}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2xy+{y}^{2}}{xy}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{{x}^{2}+2xy}{xy}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j})+(-\\frac{{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}y}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{x{y}^{2}}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2x+y}{x}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{x+2y}{y}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j})+(-\\frac{x+y}{x}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{x+y}{y}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{x+y}{x}+\\frac{2x+y}{x})\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+(-\\frac{x+y}{y}+\\frac{x+2y}{y})\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338958 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{q}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338959 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{q}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186339107 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2xy+9 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2xy-9 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2xy-9$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,2)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{t}(-1,2)={f}_{y}(-1,2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(-1,2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf{t}(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(-1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2xy-9)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2xy-9)\\,j \\\\  & =(2y-2x)\\,i-(2x-2y)\\,j \\\\ t(-1,2) & =(2(2)-2(-1))\\,i-(2(-1)-2(2))\\,j \\\\  & =6\\,i+6\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $6\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+6\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$"] [17592186339110 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2xy & ={x}^{2}+8 \\\\ {y}^{2}+2xy-{x}^{2} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={y}^{2}+2xy-{x}^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=8.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{t}(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf{t}(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{t}(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+2xy-{x}^{2})\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+2xy-{x}^{2})\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =2(y+x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2(y-x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $\\mathbf{j}$, we must have $\\mathbf{t}\\cdot \\mathbf{j}=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{t}(x,y)\\cdot \\mathbf{j} & =0 \\\\ (2(y+x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2(y-x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\mathbf{j} & =0 \\\\ -2(y-x) & =0 \\\\ y & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=x$ into the equation of our curve gives:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2xy-{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ (x)^{2}+2x(x)-{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{x}^{2}-{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- If $x=2$, we get $y=2$ and the corresponding point is $(2,2).$\n\n- If $x=-2$, we get $y=-2$ and the corresponding point is $(-2,-2).$"] [17592186339115 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2xy & =-5y+4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2xy+5y & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+2xy+5y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=4.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{t}(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf{t}(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{t}(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2xy+5y)\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2xy+5y)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(2x+5)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-(2x+2y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $\\mathbf{j}$, we must have $\\mathbf{t}\\cdot \\mathbf{j}=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{t}(x,y)\\cdot \\mathbf{j} & =0 \\\\ ((2x+5)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-(2x+2y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\mathbf{j} & =0 \\\\ -(2x+2y) & =0 \\\\ x & =-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-y$ into the equation of our curve gives:    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2xy+5y & =4 \\\\ (-y)^{2}+2(-y)y+5y & =4 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2{y}^{2}+5y & =4 \\\\ -{y}^{2}+5y-4 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-5y+4 & =0 \\\\ (y-1)(y-4) & =0 \\\\ y & =1\\,\\text{or}\\,y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- If $y=1$, we get $x=-1$ and the corresponding point is $(-1,1).$\n\n- If $y=4$, we get $x=-4$ and the corresponding point is $(-4,4).$"] [17592186339122 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4xy & =1-3x \\\\ 2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4xy+3x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4xy+3x$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{t}(1,1)={f}_{y}(1,1)\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(1,1)\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf{t}(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4xy+3x)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4xy+3x)j \\\\  & =(4y{x}^{3}-4x)i-(6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4y+3)j \\\\ t(1,1) & =(4(1)(1)^{3}-4(1))i-(6(1)^{2}(1)^{2}-4(1)+3)j \\\\  & =-5\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $-5\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$"] [17592186341845 "First, we factor the expression:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}x+2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}x+2{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x\\operatorname{cos}x=\\operatorname{sin}x+2\\operatorname{cos}x({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x)\n$$\n\nThen, we use ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}x+2\\operatorname{cos}x({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x) & =\\operatorname{sin}x+2\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}x+2\\operatorname{cos}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}x+2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}x+2{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x\\operatorname{cos}x=\\operatorname{sin}x+2\\operatorname{cos}x.\n$$"] [17592186341846 "First, we factor $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ from the first two terms:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}x+\\operatorname{sin}x{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+1}{\\operatorname{cos}x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x)+1}{\\operatorname{cos}x}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x)+1}{\\operatorname{cos}x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x+1}{\\operatorname{cos}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}x+\\operatorname{sec}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}x+\\operatorname{sin}x{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+1}{\\operatorname{cos}x}=\\operatorname{tan}x+\\operatorname{sec}x\n$$"] [17592186341847 "First, we rewrite the second term using the definition of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$\n\nWe put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x} & =\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{cos}x)\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x}+\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x+({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x)}{\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x+1}{\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$"] [17592186341858 "Let's start by finding the area of $△ABD.$ We get \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{\\mathrm{△}ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot AB\\cdot AD\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 9\\cdot 15\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{52}^{∘} \\\\  & =67.5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{52}^{∘} \\\\  & =53.190… \\\\  & \\approx 53{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nThe difference of the areas of $△ABD$ and the quadrilateral $ABCD$ gives the area of $△BCD.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{\\mathrm{△}BCD} & ={\\mathcal{A}}_{\\mathrm{△}ABD}-{\\mathcal{A}}_{ABCD} \\\\  & =53-34 \\\\  & =19{\\text{ cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}$$"] [17592186341859 "The diagonal $\\overset{―}{AC}$ divides the parallelogram into two congruent triangles. Therefore, the area of the parallelogram is twice the area of $△ACB$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/7c/7cfcb5cdf77b4afbf25afd64e37bb33f0ab2c211e09659181bfc16db0ccd2db7.png)\n\n Computing the area of $△ACB,$ we find\n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{ACD} & =\\frac{1}{2}(18)(8)\\operatorname{sin}{70}^{∘} \\\\  & =72\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{70}^{∘} \\\\  & \\approx 67.658,\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. Consequently,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{ABCD} & =2{\\mathcal{A}}_{ACB} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 67.658 \\\\  & =135.316.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, to the nearest integer, the area of the parallelogram is $135.$"] [17592186341976 "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot (-3))\\mathbf{i}-(2\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{j}+(2\\cdot (-3)-(-1)\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-3,-3,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨1,-3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,-3,-3⟩ \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-3)+(-3)\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186341977 "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 4-2\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{i}-((-1)\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{j}+((-1)\\cdot (-1)-3\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =14\\mathbf{i}+8\\mathbf{j}-5\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨14,8,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨-2,4,3⟩\\cdot ⟨14,8,-5⟩ \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 14+4\\cdot 8+3\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186341978 "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot 4-0\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{i}-((-2)\\cdot 4-0\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{j}+((-2)\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-4\\mathbf{i}+8\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-4,8,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨-2,0,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨-4,8,1⟩ \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-4)+0\\cdot 8+(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186341979 "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{q}\\cdot (\\mathbf{r}\\times \\mathbf{p})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{q},$ $\\mathbf{r},$ and $\\mathbf{p}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{q}\\cdot (\\mathbf{r}\\times \\mathbf{p}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2((-2)\\cdot 1-4\\cdot (-2))+(1\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =12-11 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186341980 "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{r}\\cdot (\\mathbf{q}\\times \\mathbf{p})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{r},$ $\\mathbf{q},$ and $\\mathbf{p}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}\\cdot (\\mathbf{q}\\times \\mathbf{p}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4((-2)\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1)-(1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 3)-2(1\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =-20+1-14 \\\\  & =-33.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186341983 "First, we compute the triple scalar product \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨4,-2,6⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot (-1)+6\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12+2+12 \\\\  & =26.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}) &=-26.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186342038 "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ 2 & \\text{ }{\\color{red}\\ge }-2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\text{ }{\\color{red}\\ge }-2 \\\\ -2 & \\le -2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le 2 \\\\ 1-2 & \\le 1-2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le 1+2 \\\\ -1 & \\le x\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-1\\le x\\le 3.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -2 & \\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 2 \\\\ -2-3 & \\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3\\le 2-3 \\\\ -5 & \\le y\\le -1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-5\\le y\\le -1.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $-1\\le x\\le 3$ and $-5\\le y\\le -1.$"] [17592186342070 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot (\\mathbf{q}\\times \\mathbf{r}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ t & t & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}t & 2 \\\\ 2 & 7\\end{vmatrix}-3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}t & 2 \\\\ -1 & 7\\end{vmatrix}+6\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}t & t \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-3(t\\cdot 7-(-1)\\cdot 2)+6(t\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot t) \\\\  & =-21t-6+12t+6t \\\\  & =-3t-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot (\\mathbf{q}\\times \\mathbf{r}) & =0 \\\\ -3t-6 & =0 \\\\ -3t & =6 \\\\ t & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $t=-2.$"] [17592186342071 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot (\\mathbf{q}\\times \\mathbf{r}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}t & 1 & t \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =t\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+t\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =t((-1)\\cdot 1-0\\cdot (-1))-(2\\cdot 1-3\\cdot (-1))+t(2\\cdot 0-3\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =-t-5+3t \\\\  & =2t-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot (\\mathbf{q}\\times \\mathbf{r}) & =0 \\\\ 2t-5 & =0 \\\\ 2t & =5 \\\\ t & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $t=\\frac{5}{2}.$"] [17592186342075 "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})-(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i} \\\\  & =⟨-3,0,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j})-(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}+1\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨2,1,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3(1\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 0)-0+0 \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-9|=9.\n$$"] [17592186342076 "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,2,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}-2\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}-4\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,4,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -4\\end{vmatrix}-3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{vmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3(2\\cdot (-4)-4\\cdot (-1))-3(0\\cdot (-4)-(-2)\\cdot (-1))+(0\\cdot 4-(-2)\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =12+6+4 \\\\  & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|22|=22.\n$$"] [17592186342077 "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})-(\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨1,0,-2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(0\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot (-2))+(1\\cdot 1-3\\cdot (-2))-(1\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =4+7+2 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|13|=13.\n$$"] [17592186342078 "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k})-(2\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(2\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,2,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(2\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨1,0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3(2\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 2)-3((-1)\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 2)-((-1)\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =-6+9+2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|\\,5\\,|=5.\n$$"] [17592186342081 "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})-(-2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =4\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨4,0,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(-2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}-2\\mathbf{k})-(-2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =5\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨5,-3,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -3 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4((-2)\\cdot (-3)-1\\cdot (-3))-0-(3\\cdot (-3)-5\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =36-1 \\\\  & =35.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|35|=35.\n$$"]}
